  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA closed low will continue to drift across the Upper Midwest\ntoday. Southerly flow ahead of this trough, and mixing, should\nresult in high temperatures around 15 to 25 degrees above\naverage, with the warmest readings across PA and nrn WV. Some\nsites could approach record highs before clouds thicken through\nthe day ahead of an approaching shortwave. This shortwave (and\nassociated cold front,) ahead of the main upper low, will \napproach the region in SW flow aloft this afternoon, crossing\nthe region this evening into early Thursday. Moisture and \nascent with the wave, assisted by a jet aloft, should result in \nrain overspreading the region from W-E this afternoon through \nthis evening. A low level jet and minimal elevated instability \ncould also result in an isolated thunderstorm. \n\nRainfall amounts are expected to range from 0.25 to 0.5 inches\nfor most areas. Low end QPF (10th percentile) is around 0.10,\nwith high end amounts (90th percentile) around 0.8 inches. \n\nThe shortwave and surface front should exit the region Thursday\nmorning, with decreasing rain chances. Temperatures will be\ncooler than recent days, though still around 10 degrees above\naverage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate the upper low will open into a trough\nThursday, as it tracks across the Great Lakes region. Another\ndeepening upper low is then progged to drop SE through the\ntrough, reaching the Great Lakes region again on Friday. This \nlow should continue eastward across the Upper Ohio Valley region\nthrough Saturday. This should spread areas of rain across the \nregion again later Friday into Saturday. 850 mb temperatures are\nalso progged to drop to between 0 and -2 deg C during this time\naccording to the latest ensembles. This should bring temperature\nback down to near seasonable levels over the weekend.",
